30. A method only needs overriding when its *behavior* differs from the parent’s; if the subclass’s version is a strict superset of the parent’s logic (e.g., same steps plus one extra step), `super().method(...)` lets it call the parent’s implementation first and then add the new behavior, avoiding duplicated code. If behavior is identical, no override is needed at all.

